hw/m68k/mcf_intc: a forced request bypasses the mask

Arbitration masked forced requests along with real ones. Per the MCF54455
interrupt controller, INTFRC is independent of IMR: a source whose mask bit
stays set for the life of the machine still reaches the CPU when software
forces it. Firmware that forces its timer tick from another ISR, and never
unmasks that source at all, otherwise runs with the tick frozen.

--- a/hw/m68k/mcf_intc.c
+++ b/hw/m68k/mcf_intc.c
@@ -42,7 +42,15 @@
     int best;
     int best_level;
 
-    active = (s->ipr | s->ifr) & s->enabled & ~s->imr;
+    /*
+     * A forced request (INTFRC) bypasses the mask. The Octatrack firmware
+     * forces source 32 (its sequencer tick) from the audio frame ISR while
+     * IMRH bit 0 stays set for the machine's whole life -- no CIMR write of
+     * 32 and no direct IMRH store exist anywhere in its image -- yet the
+     * sequencer demonstrably runs on hardware. Masking forced requests
+     * leaves the transport playing with the step clock frozen.
+     */
+    active = ((s->ipr & ~s->imr) | s->ifr) & s->enabled;
     best_level = 0;
     best = 64;
     if (active) {
